{"answer":"Let $f(x) = x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1$. Using Taylor's expansion in powers of $(x-1)$:\n$$f(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) + \\frac{f''(1)}{2!}(x-1)^2 + \\frac{f'''(1)}{3!}(x-1)^3 + \\frac{f^{(iv)}(1)}{4!}(x-1)^4 + \\frac{f^{(v)}(1)}{5!}(x-1)^5$$\n\nCalculating values at $x = 1$:\n- $f(x) = x^5 - x^4 + x^3 - x^2 + x - 1 \\implies f(1) = 1 - 1 + 1 - 1 + 1 - 1 = 0$\n- $f'(x) = 5x^4 - 4x^3 + 3x^2 - 2x + 1 \\implies f'(1) = 5 - 4 + 3 - 2 + 1 = 3$\n- $f''(x) = 20x^3 - 12x^2 + 6x - 2 \\implies f''(1) = 20 - 12 + 6 - 2 = 12$\n- $f'''(x) = 60x^2 - 24x + 6 \\implies f'''(1) = 60 - 24 + 6 = 42$\n- $f^{(iv)}(x) = 120x - 24 \\implies f^{(iv)}(1) = 120 - 24 = 96$\n- $f^{(v)}(x) = 120 \\implies f^{(v)}(1) = 120$\n\nSubstituting values:\n$$f(x) = 0 + 3(x-1) + \\frac{12}{2!}(x-1)^2 + \\frac{42}{3!}(x-1)^3 + \\frac{96}{4!}(x-1)^4 + \\frac{120}{5!}(x-1)^5$$\n$$f(x) = 3(x-1) + 6(x-1)^2 + 7(x-1)^3 + 4(x-1)^4 + (x-1)^5$$\n\nTo find $f(0.99)$, substitute $x = 0.99$, so $(x-1) = -0.01$:\n$$f(0.99) = 3(-0.01) + 6(-0.01)^2 + 7(-0.01)^3 + 4(-0.01)^4 + (-0.01)^5$$\n$$f(0.99) = -0.03 + 0.0006 - 0.000007 + 0.00000004 - 0.0000000001 = -0.02940696$$\nHence, $f(0.99) \\approx -0.0294$.","proTip":"Synthetic division can be used as an alternative rapid method to Taylor's expansion for polynomials.","videoLinks":[{"title":"Taylor series expansion of polynomial","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Taylor+series+expansion+of+polynomial"},{"title":"Taylor theorem powers of x-a","url":"https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=Taylor+theorem+powers+of+x-a"}]}